          "Engineers sometimes accidentally hardcode `6 * N * D` while ignoring embedding lookups, rotary position math, or attention FLOPs at very long context sizes (`seq_len >= 32k`). While `6 * N * D` holds accurately at standard 2k–8k pre-training lengths where dense linear projections dominate attention quadratic FLOPs (`2 * B * H * S^2`), extremely long contexts shift additional quadratic FLOP overhead onto self-attention layers. Furthermore, sparse Mixture-of-Experts architectures count active parameters per token rather than total parameters inside `N`. Recognizing `compute_training_flops` as the dense transformer baseline ensures teams add sequence or sparsity corrections when sizing specialized models.",
