          "Breaking Ring All-Reduce down through `RING_ALLREDUCE_TAXONOMY` clarifies how peer-to-peer ring messaging removes central server bottlenecks. In naive parameter-server architectures, one root coordinator receives gradients from all `N - 1` workers and sends updated parameters back, overloading its network interface linearly as cluster size grows. By organizing `N` GPUs into a directed cycle (`RING_TOPOLOGY`), every rank exchanges only with two logical neighbors—receiving from `(rank - 1) % N` while concurrently sending to `(rank + 1) % N`—so every interconnect link carries full duplex traffic simultaneously.",
          "Understanding both collective stages highlights why total per-GPU volume remains bounded independent of cluster scale. During `'SCATTER_REDUCE'`, tensors split into `N` equal partitions (`S / N` bytes each); across `N - 1` ring hops, each GPU accumulates incoming slices element-wise until GPU `k` holds the globally reduced sum of chunk `k`. Immediately afterward, `'ALL_GATHER'` executes `N - 1` additional circular shifts where each rank forwards finalized chunks around the loop without arithmetic until every worker owns the complete global result. Combining both halves produces `2 * (N - 1) / N * S` bytes transferred per worker (`'BANDWIDTH_OPTIMALITY'`).",
