          "Understanding exact failure modes triggered by those `5` vs `8` exponent bits saves days of silent convergence debugging. Under pure `FP16`, small gradient magnitudes below `6.1e-5` (common in deep transformer attention tails during mid-to-late training) drop silently into zero (underflow), whereas large RMSNorm or residual spikes past `65504.0` convert instantly into `+inf` / `NaN` (overflow). Rescuing `FP16` therefore forces trainers to wrap every backward pass inside Dynamic Loss Scaling, tracking overflow steps and retrying skipped gradient steps cleanly—complexity that `'BF16'` drops completely on Ampere (`A100`) and Hopper (`H100`) silicon.",
