          "Examining failure cases highlights both the power and risk of predicting scales across `amax_history` windows (typically 16–1024 steps). During stable pre-training, layer activation magnitudes drift gently by only ~1–3% per iteration, so delayed scaling hits optimal precision continuously with zero extra memory reads (`0` HBM overhead!). However, if an unusual prompt spike causes next step’s `amax` to jump abruptly from `16.0` up to `25.0`, applying stale `scale = 26.6` multiplies peak activations to `665.0 > 448.0`, clamping outliers against E4M3’s `448.0` ceiling or introducing transient `NaN`s until the `amax` ring buffer updates on the very next iteration.",
