        "title": "MoE Routing — Core Algorithm & State Representation",
        "say": [
          "In static computational graphs like those on GPUs and TPUs, dynamic tensor shapes with variable token counts induce severe compilation and allocation overhead.",
          "To maintain static tensor shapes, distributed MoE implementations define a fixed buffer size called expert capacity.",
          "Expert capacity is calculated as: ceil((num_tokens * top_k / num_experts) * capacity_factor).",
          "The capacity factor is a slack multiplier (typically between 1.0 and 1.5) that provides headroom for non-uniform expert routing distributions.",
          "If tokens are distributed perfectly evenly across all experts, a capacity factor of 1.0 accommodates all tokens with zero drops.",
          "However, because gating networks naturally develop routing preferences, capacity factors greater than 1.0 are required in practice to prevent dropped tokens.",
          "Hardware accelerators execute matrix multiplications with peak efficiency when tensor buffer dimensions are known at kernel launch time.",
          "Pre-allocating expert capacity buffers eliminates runtime GPU memory reallocation and prevents fragmented memory layouts in High Bandwidth Memory.",
          "Performance engineers benchmark different capacity factor settings to balance memory overhead against dropped token rates.",
          "Holding expert buffer shapes constant enables reliable CUDA graph capture across training steps.",
          "Fixed static tensor shapes demanded by XLA, `torch.compile`, and Tensor Core GEMM launchers require every expert buffer to pre-allocate an invariant uniform token budget sized via `simulate_expert_capacity`. Computing `math.ceil((num_tokens * top_k / num_experts) * capacity_factor)` translates expected average traffic per expert into concrete buffer row slots! Evaluating `(num_tokens=2048, num_experts=8, top_k=2, capacity_factor=1.25)` places mean load at `2048 * 2 / 8 = 512` tokens per expert, then multiplies by the `25%` headroom cushion (`1.25`) to yield `640` static buffer slots per expert.",
          "Inspecting why `'capacity_factor'` behaves like a structural slack valve clarifies the hardware cost-versus-quality dilemma inside fixed-shape MoE implementations. Setting `capacity_factor=1.0` assumes every batch distributes uniformly across all `8` experts (`512` tokens each)—which almost never holds instantaneously within finite 2k-token batches! Slight natural fluctuations pushing Expert 2 to `560` tokens and Expert 5 to `470` cause Expert 2 to silently overflow its buffer and discard the excess `48` tokens (token dropping), hurting perplexity even though `Expert 5` sits partially empty.",
          "Cranking `'capacity_factor'` steeply upward (`e.g., 2.0` -> `1024` buffer slots per expert) slashes token drops near zero, yet extracts twin hardware penalties across GPU HBM and Tensor Cores. Every padded slot up to capacity consumes linear intermediate SwiGLU activation memory (`capacity * mlp_hidden`) AND forces grouped matrix multiplications to crunch zero-padded dummy rows (`50%` wasted FLOPs when buffers stay half-empty)! Typical production mixes run `1.25` during heavy pre-training or `1.5–2.0` during delicate validation/fine-tuning—or deploy dropless ragged Megablocks kernels (`block-sparse GEMMs`) wherever dynamic variable sizes remain supported.",
          "Calibrating capacity budgets deterministically inside `'simulate_expert_capacity'` before allocating cluster MoE communication buffers stops hard `cudaErrorIllegalAddress` index out-of-bounds kernel panics and guarantees uniform expert tensor layouts across pipeline stages.",
          "Buffer alignment arithmetic inside `simulate_expert_capacity` (`2048, 8, 2, 1.25 -> 640`) underscores how fixed hardware shapes accelerate grouped Tensor Core execution (`torch._grouped_mm`). Static uniform buffers keep matrix multiply dimensions (`[640, hidden]`) cleanly aligned to 64/128 tensor-tile multiples across XLA compilers without dynamic recompilation pauses.",
          "Dropless sparse block kernels (`MegaBlocks`) eliminate hard capacity drop ceilings altogether whenever ragged GPU Tensor Core schedulers remain available—grouping incoming routed tokens cleanly into variable `128x128` sparse blocks with zero dropped inputs and eliminates zero-padded dead buffer compute.",
          "Choosing between fixed-capacity buffers versus block-sparse MegaBlocks depends primarily on compiler constraints and cluster support for dynamic grouped GEMM kernels.",
          "Selecting between fixed padded capacity matrices versus block-sparse MegaBlocks kernels balances compiler XLA uniformity directly against eliminating wasted zero-pad compute inside conditional Mixture-of-Experts FFNs.",
          "Evaluating fixed static buffer sizing directly against ragged block-sparse MegaBlocks kernels guides systems engineers on trading XLA compiler regularity cleanly against zero-drop token coverage in production Mixture-of-Experts layers.",
          "This structural predictability ensures that distributed MoE layers achieve consistent, deterministic execution timing across iterations."
        ],
        "example": "A restaurant dining room reserving 25 tables per waiter: a capacity factor of 1.25 allows up to 31 guests per section during dinner rushes.",
        "code": "import math\n\ndef simulate_expert_capacity(num_tokens: int, num_experts: int, top_k: int, capacity_factor: float) -> dict:\n    avg_tokens_per_expert = (num_tokens * top_k) / num_experts\n    expert_capacity = math.ceil(avg_tokens_per_expert * capacity_factor)\n    return {\n        \"num_tokens\": num_tokens,\n        \"num_experts\": num_experts,\n        \"top_k\": top_k,\n        \"capacity_factor\": capacity_factor,\n        \"expert_capacity\": expert_capacity\n    }\n\nres = simulate_expert_capacity(num_tokens=2048, num_experts=8, top_k=2, capacity_factor=1.25)\nprint(f\"MoE Capacity: Tokens={res['num_tokens']}, Experts={res['num_experts']} -> Capacity per expert={res['expert_capacity']}\")",
